  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="CVN" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="40.30242804622567" Magnitude="0" Name="Field surrounding Cloud-9" Opacity="100" RA="12.864220354333506" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/heic2601c/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.25348435821506843">
    <Description>A team using the NASA/ESA Hubble Space Telescope has uncovered a new type of astronomical object —a starless, gas-rich, dark-matter cloud that is considered a “relic” or remnant of early galaxy formation. Nicknamed “Cloud-9,” this is the first confirmed detection of such an object in the Universe This image shows the blank field of the surrounding region of Cloud-9. The image identifying its location can be found here. [Image description: A dark field with stars and galaxies of various sizes speckled throughout the image. A particularly bright star is visible in the upper left region of the image.]</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.05686668480933949" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="192.9633048747402" CenterY="40.302418234874835" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Field surrounding Cloud-9" OffsetX="6.941733985515075e-06" OffsetY="6.941733985515075e-06" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="46.9599999999997" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/heic2601c/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcorepipe_ids="hst|heic2601c">
