  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="LYN" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="36.05704208129255" Magnitude="0" Name="A Cosmic Cobweb" Opacity="100" RA="8.015776904567293" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/heic2213a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.34709354228286193">
    <Description>In celebration of Halloween, Hubble brings you this inky image of the galaxy cluster Abell 611, located roughly 3.2 billion light years from Earth. This object is a popular target for investigating dark matter, in part because of the numerous examples of strong gravitational lensing visible amongst the cluster’s intricate web of galaxies. [Image Description: Hundreds of small galaxies appear across this view. Their colours vary. Some are shades of orange, while others are white. Most appear as fuzzy ovals, but a few have distinct spiral arms. There are also many thin, long, orange arcs that curve around the centre of the image, where there is a prominent orange glow.]</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.06830770760677662" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="120.23666079324872" CenterY="36.0570412749835" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="A Cosmic Cobweb" OffsetX="4.169171606858925e-06" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-37.14000000000016" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/heic2213a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcorepipe_ids="hst|heic2213a">
        <Credits>ESA/Hubble, NASA, P. Kelly, M. Postman, J. Richard, S. Allen</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://esahubble.org/images/heic2213a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/heic2213a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
