  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="ERI" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-8.580639050989138" Magnitude="0" Name="Ring of Stellar Wildfire" Opacity="100" RA="4.566680934969915" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/potw2032a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.3732942450238007">
    <Description>NGC 1614, captured here by the NASA/ESA Hubble Space Telescope, is an eccentrically-shaped galaxy ablaze with activity. The galaxy resides about 200 million light-years from Earth and is nestled in the southern constellation of Eridanus (The River). NGC 1614 is the result of a past galactic merger which created its peculiar appearance. The cosmic collision also drove a turbulent flow of interstellar gas from the smaller of the two galaxies involved into the nucleus of the larger one, resulting in a burst of star formation which started in the core and slowly spread outwards through the galaxy. Owing to its turbulent past and its current appearance, astronomers classify NGC 1614 as a peculiar galaxy, a starburst galaxy, and a luminous infrared galaxy. Luminous infrared galaxies are among the most luminous objects in the local Universe — and NGC 1614 is, in fact, the second most luminous galaxy within 250 million light-years.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.0450701315723264" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="68.5002164352" CenterY="-8.5806316445" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Ring of Stellar Wildfire" OffsetX="5.50172504545e-06" OffsetY="5.50172504545e-06" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-117.15999999999994" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/hst/potw2032a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcorepipe_ids="hst|potw2032a">
